<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chaghri Beg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chaghri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chaghri Beg was a grandson of Seljuk and brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a>, with whom he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> in the early eleventh century. The brothers captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> and defeated the Ghaznavids at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dandanaqan">Battle of Dandanaqan</a> in 1040. The victory secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> to the Seljuk Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chaghri-beg.jpg" alt="Chaghri Beg" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:LouisAragon (uploader) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Ahmad_Qavurt,_minted_in_Jiroft.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
